{{History of Balisca}}
The '''Carpinchera Republic''' ({{Langx|an|República de Carpinchera}}), also known as the '''Chigüire Republic''' or the '''State of Macaio''', was an [[W:List of historical unrecognized states#Americas|unrecognized]] [[W:Breakaway state|breakaway state]] from the secessionist [[Xunta Nacionalista]], that for 14 months (1828-1829) militarily controlled an area consisting of the settlement of [[Macaio]] and it's immediate hinterland. 

Tensions regarding the [[Confederal Question]] had been simmering across the [[Southern Confederation]] since the end of the [[Pastry War]], especially so in Bayara as a result of the presence of the [[Cerdanyan Legion]] in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian lowlands]]. This presence was viewed by most rural Bayarans as an illegitimate military occupation. Nationalist sentiment ran high across the republic, with the exception of urban Macaio, which economically dominated the Republic and generally favored [[Confederal emergency|confederal intervention]]. Officials in Macaio were aware of the imminent Bayaran renunciation of the [[Articles of Confederation]], and became the only Bayaran territory to send a delegation to the [[1827 Confederal Convention]]. The rebellion was covertly encouraged by Hesperian trade and business interests in Macaio, motivated by the city's status as a major [[W:Entrepôt|entrepôt]].

The Carpinchera Republic was never explicitly recognized by the Confederation; it remained, however, independent under its own government and constitution, and played a key role in disrupting the secessionist war effort. Carpinchera and the rest of Bayara rejoined the Southern Confederation after the former's victory at the [[Battle of Punta Lagunas]] in 1837.

